 "notes": [
  "The floating-pin warning on pin 2 is not a real problem here, so I kept the graph as it was. The tutorial has no external resistor in its parts list or circuit image. Its sketch calls pinMode(2, INPUT_PULLUP), so the Leonardo's internal pull-up holds pin 2 HIGH while the button is open. Pressing the button connects pin 2 to GND and it reads LOW. Adding a resistor would go against the rule to use exactly the tutorial's parts.",
  "The button straddles the centre gap. Side 1 (1.l, 1.r) is in the top half at columns 10 and 12. Side 2 (2.l, 2.r) is in the bottom half at the same columns.",
  "The blue wire runs from pin 2 to column 10 top, which is side 1 of the button. The black wire runs from GND.1 to column 10 bottom, which is side 2.",
  "The circuit image crops the black wire at the bottom. I assumed it ends on the button's bottom half, as shown just under the button.",
  "The exact breadboard columns and rows are my choice, because the image does not show them precisely."
 ],
